    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair.jpg" alt="Harald Fairhair" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flateyjarbok_Haraldr_Halfdan_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jón Þórðarson and Magnús Þórhallsson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> grew out of the many chiefdoms and petty kingdoms that lined the western Scandinavian coast in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. Its founding is traditionally credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, who according to the sagas defeated a coalition of rival kings at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hafrsfjord">Battle of Hafrsfjord</a> near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavanger">Stavanger</a>, an event conventionally dated around 872 but placed by many historians somewhat later, perhaps in the 880s. Harald's authority was probably limited to the western and southern coast, while the earls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lade-trondheim">Lade</a> in Trøndelag and the chieftains of the inland districts kept much of their independence. The main sources, skaldic poems and the kings' sagas written down in the thirteenth century, mix history with legend, so the story of unification remains uncertain in its details. Real consolidation came with the missionary kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good">Haakon the Good</a>, raised at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan of England</a>, ruled in the mid-tenth century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> (r. 995–1000) forced conversion on the coastal districts until his death at the Battle of Svolder; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a> (r. 1015–1028) completed the work of Christianisation. Driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a>, Olaf Haraldsson returned and was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stiklestad">Battle of Stiklestad</a> in 1030, and his rapid veneration as Saint Olaf gave the monarchy a sacred patron and a lasting focus of loyalty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergen.jpg" alt="Bergen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bergen_panorama_at_night_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TomasEE, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s narrow strips of farmland, deep fjords and long coastline produced a society of scattered farms rather than villages, in which free farmers (<em>bønder</em>) owned much of the land and met in regional assemblies. Below them stood tenants, servants and, until the twelfth or thirteenth century, thralls. Farming of barley, oats and livestock was combined with hunting, fishing and summer pasturing in the mountains. The decisive export was stockfish, cod dried on racks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lofoten">Lofoten</a> islands and shipped south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, which became the kingdom's largest town. From the late thirteenth century German merchants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> dominated this trade, and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontor">Kontor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryggen">Bryggen</a> in Bergen, established around 1360, controlled the exchange of fish for Baltic grain. The Church, organised under the archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Nidaros</a> (Trondheim) from 1152–1153, built stone cathedrals and stave churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urnes-stave-church">Urnes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgund-stave-church">Borgund</a> and became the largest landowner. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1349 and later plagues killed perhaps half the population and left many farms deserted for generations. In the early modern period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> exports, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongsberg">Kongsberg</a> (from 1623) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roros">Røros</a> (from 1644) added new wealth, and since the discovery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekofisk">Ekofisk</a> field in 1969 oil and gas have made Norway one of the richest countries in the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder.jpg" alt="Battle of Svolder" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder">Battle of Svolder</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svolder,_by_Otto_Sinding.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Otto Sinding, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Viking-age Norwegian power rested on ships. Kings raised fleets through the <em>leidang</em>, a levy in which coastal districts each equipped and crewed a warship, and the skaldic poems celebrate sea battles such as Hafrsfjord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-svolder">Svolder</a> in 1000 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nesjar">Nesjar</a> in 1016, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a> defeated Earl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-haakonsson">Svein Haakonsson</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> (r. 1046–1066), a veteran of the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>, fought a long war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> before invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stamford-bridge">Battle of Stamford Bridge</a> in 1066. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-barefoot">Magnus Barefoot</a> campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, Man and Ireland and died in Ulster in 1103. From 1130 to 1240 the kingdom was torn by civil wars between rival claimants and factions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagler">Bagler</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sverre-of-norway">Sverre Sigurdsson</a> secured his throne with a sea victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fimreite">Fimreite</a> in 1184. The overseas realm was tested in 1263, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV</a> led a great fleet to defend the Hebrides; after the inconclusive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a> he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. Under Danish kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> supplied sailors and soldiers, and its border fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-fredriksten">Fredriksten</a> saw the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> in 1718. In April 1940 the guns and torpedoes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscarsborg-fortress">Oscarsborg</a> sank the German cruiser <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-cruiser-blucher">Blücher</a></em>, delaying the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, but the country was occupied until May 1945.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good.jpg" alt="Haakon the Good" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good">Haakon the Good</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Nicolai_Arbo-Haakon_den_gode.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peter Nicolai Arbo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval Norwegian kingship was shaped by regional assemblies, the <em>things</em>, where free men met to make law and acclaim kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulating">Gulating</a> in the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frostating">Frostating</a> in Trøndelag had their own law books, traditionally associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good">Haakon the Good</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsivating">Eidsivating</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgarting">Borgarting</a> served the east. Royal power grew in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries with the support of the Church: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson">Magnus Erlingsson</a> was crowned in 1163 or 1164, the first coronation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, and a succession law of 1260 established hereditary monarchy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV</a> (r. 1217–1263) built a royal administration of district officials (<em>sysselmenn</em>) and chancery clerks, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway">Magnus VI</a>, called the Lawmender, issued a national law code (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-lagabotes-land-law">Landsloven</a></em>) in 1274 and a town law soon after, among the earliest codes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> valid for a whole kingdom. Haakon V moved the centre of government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, where he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akershus-fortress">Akershus Fortress</a>. In union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, often through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> (<em>stattholder</em>), and after 1660 under absolute monarchy. The constitution adopted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> on 17 May 1814, among the most liberal of its time, created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a>, Norway's parliament; parliamentary government was established in 1884 and women won the vote on equal terms in 1913.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows.jpg" alt="L'Anse aux Meadows" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L'Anse aux Meadows</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Authentic_Viking_recreation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dylan Kereluk from White Rock, Canada, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> expansion across the North Atlantic. Norwegians settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, Iceland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and Man, raided and settled in Ireland and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and from Iceland reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and, around 1000, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, where the Norse site at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L'Anse aux Meadows</a> has been excavated. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway">Magnus VI</a> the Norwegian crown ruled the largest realm in its history, from Greenland to the Baltic approaches, after Greenland and Iceland submitted in 1261–1264. Much of what is known about early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> comes from the kings' sagas written in Iceland, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a></em>, composed around 1230, while the Norwegian court sponsored translations of French romances and produced the mirror for princes <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konungs-skuggsja">Konungs skuggsjá</a></em>. The cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Saint Olaf</a> spread across the Nordic world and beyond, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a> a major pilgrimage destination. Modern Norwegian national identity was built on this medieval past and on nineteenth-century culture: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrik-ibsen">Henrik Ibsen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-grieg">Edvard Grieg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-munch">Edvard Munch</a> won international fame, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fridtjof-nansen">Fridtjof Nansen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roald-amundsen">Roald Amundsen</a> led polar exploration, Amundsen reaching the South Pole in 1911. Since 1945 Norway has been a founding member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, a major aid donor and peace mediator, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> is awarded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars.jpg" alt="Napoleonic Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoleonic_Wars_(revision).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The medieval kingdom weakened after 1319, when Haakon V died without a son and his infant grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-eriksson">Magnus Eriksson</a> inherited both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1349 cut royal and church revenues sharply, and in 1380 the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vi-of-norway">Haakon VI</a> left the crown to his son Olaf, already king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, beginning more than four centuries of union. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a> Norway entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> in 1397, and in 1468–1469 Christian I pledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> as security for his daughter's dowry; they were never redeemed. In 1536–1537 Christian III imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a>, the last archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Nidaros</a> fled, and the Norwegian council of the realm disappeared; Norway remained a kingdom in name but was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>. Defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, Denmark ceded Norway to Sweden by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> in January 1814. The Norwegians answered with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> constitution and a brief war, then accepted union with Sweden under their own constitution in November 1814. On 7 June 1905 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> dissolved the union, and the Danish prince Carl became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vii">Haakon VII</a>. After the German occupation of 1940–1945 Norway rebuilt itself as a social democratic welfare state; its voters rejected membership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Community</a> in 1972 and 1994, and it is today a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald</a> V, king since 1991.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> matters as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> expansion across the North Atlantic, as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s oldest kingdoms with an early national law code, and as a state that survived four centuries of union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> to re-emerge between 1814 and 1905 as a democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, now among the world's wealthiest.</em></p>

